  "intro_html": "<p><strong>Important Note:</strong> This problem set teaches game theory concepts as tools for understanding institutional problems, not as mathematical exercises. Focus on what the analysis reveals about human cooperation and informal institutional design.</p>\n<p><strong>Instructions:</strong> Throughout this problem set, you'll see:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>[READ]</strong> = Provided information/example to study</li>\n<li><strong>[DO]</strong> = Calculate or fill in this answer yourself</li>\n<li><strong>[WRITE]</strong> = Write an explanation (2-3 sentences unless otherwise specified)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Submission Guidelines</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What to submit:</strong> All filled-in calculations ([DO]) and written responses ([THINK AND/OR WRITE])</li>\n<li><strong>Collaboration:</strong> Work with others or any other resource to understand concepts and learn to answer the questions, but write individual responses</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> Est. Budget 6-8 hours; complete Part I thoroughly before moving on</li>\n<li><strong>Due:</strong> Friday Week 3, 23:59 (grace period until Sunday 23:59)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Worked Example: Understanding Strategic Form Games</h2>\n<p>[READ] <strong>Read this complete example before starting the problems:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Two students must decide whether to study hard or slack off for a group project. They care about their grade but also about not working harder than necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Identify the Strategic Elements</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Players: Student A, Student B</li>\n<li>Strategies: Study Hard, Slack Off</li>\n<li>Payoffs: Based on grade received and effort expended</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Construct the Payoff Matrix</strong> If both study hard → good grade, but high effort → payoff = 3 each. If both slack → poor grade, low effort → payoff = 1 each. If one studies, one slacks → mixed grade, unequal effort → studier gets 0, slacker gets 4</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Student B: Study Hard</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Student B: Slack Off</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Student A: Study Hard</strong></td>\n<td>(3,3)</td>\n<td>(0,4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Student A: Slack Off</strong></td>\n<td>(4,0)</td>\n<td>(1,1)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Find Dominant Strategies</strong> For Student A: If B studies hard, A gets 4 from slacking vs. 3 from studying → slack better. If B slacks off, A gets 1 from slacking vs. 0 from studying → slack better. Slacking dominates studying for A (and by symmetry, for B too).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Predict the Outcome</strong> Both will slack off, getting payoffs of (1,1), even though both studying hard would give (3,3).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5: Institutional Insight</strong> This shows how rationally maximizing individual behavior can produce collectively rather unmaximized outcomes—the foundation for understanding why we rely on shared institutions (both informal and formal) to align individual and collective interests.</p>\n<p>But, foreshadowing next week, we also need to think about - <em>a la</em> North 1990 from IGD - the transaction costs involved with establishing and maintaining these. Together, we understand both why much of human history wasn't a 'war of all against all' as Hobbes suggested, as informal rules will emerge quickly, but also why highly organized orderly societies have been so historically rare and fragile in human history, as well as why technology that lowers various transaction costs have both aided the development of these societies, but may also threaten them. Themes I hope emerge from the problem set to follow.</p>\n<h2>Structure</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Part I: Foundation - Building Game Theory Skills</strong> (90 minutes) — Questions 1–6</li>\n<li><strong>Part II: Market Failures and Informal Solutions</strong> (90 minutes) — Questions 7–8</li>\n<li><strong>Part III: Collective Action and Informal Institutions</strong> (120 minutes) — Questions 9–12</li>\n<li><strong>Part IV: Synthesis - When Are Formal Institutions Essential?</strong> (60 minutes) — Questions 13–15</li>\n</ul>",
