    "title": "Question 3: Introducing Parameters - Social Norms",
    "subtitle": "(Lightly Guided)",
    "read_html": "<h3>Teaching Box: Understanding Parameters in Games</h3>\n<p>[READ] Parameters are variables that affect payoffs but aren't chosen by players. They represent institutional features like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>s</strong> = social cost of norm violation</li>\n<li><strong>p</strong> = probability of detection/enforcement</li>\n<li><strong>f</strong> = fine or penalty size</li>\n<li><strong>δ</strong> = discount factor (how much future payoffs matter)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By varying parameters, we can find \"critical thresholds\" - specific values where the entire equilibrium of the game shifts. For example, if social disapproval becomes strong enough (s gets large), it can flip behavior from defection to cooperation.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Return to the irrigation farmers. Now suppose the community has a norm against aggressive water use, creating social disapproval worth -s units for anyone who uses water aggressively.</p>\n<p>[READ] <strong>Part A: Payoff Matrix with Parameters</strong> The payoffs from Question 2 are modified by subtracting s from anyone choosing Aggressive:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Farmer B: Aggressive</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Farmer B: Moderate</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Farmer A: Aggressive</strong></td>\n<td>(10-s, 4-s)</td>\n<td>(10-s, 0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Farmer A: Moderate</strong></td>\n<td>(6, 10-s)</td>\n<td>(6, 6)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>",
