    "title": "Question 4: Reputation in Repeated Interaction",
    "subtitle": "(Calculation Practice)",
    "read_html": "<h3>Teaching Box: Calculating Present Values in Repeated Games</h3>\n<p>[READ] When payoffs occur over time, we need to calculate their present value using discount factors.</p>\n<p><strong>Simple Example:</strong> If you receive $100 next year and the discount factor is δ = 0.9:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present value = $100 × 0.9 = $90</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Multiple Periods:</strong> If you receive $100 every year for 3 years:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present value = $100 + $100(0.9) + $100(0.9)² = $100 + $90 + $81 = $271</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Finite Series:</strong> If you receive $100 every year for n years:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present value = $100 × (1-δⁿ)/(1-δ)</li>\n<li>Example with δ = 0.9 for 10 years: $100 × (1-0.9¹⁰)/(1-0.9) = $100 × 6.513 = $651.30</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Infinite Series:</strong> If you receive $100 every year forever:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present value = $100/(1-0.9) = $100/0.1 = $1000</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Alternating Payoffs:</strong> If you receive $100 in odd years only (year 1, 3, 5...):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present value = $100(1 + δ² + δ⁴ + ...) = $100/(1-δ²)</li>\n<li>With δ = 0.9: = $100/(1-0.81) = $100/0.19 = $526</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p>Two competing fishing companies must decide each season whether to fish a profitable area. The area can sustain one company well ($30,000 profit) but if both fish there, overfishing means each loses $10,000. They interact for 10 years with discount factor δ = 0.9.</p>\n<p><strong>Part A: Strategy Design</strong> [READ] Consider this alternating strategy: \"Company A fishes the area in odd years, Company B in even years. If anyone deviates from this pattern, both companies fish the area forever after (mutual punishment).\"</p>",
