      "label": "Part E: The Role of the Future",
      "kind": "WRITE",
      "prompt_html": "<p>Why does repeated interaction enable cooperation when one-shot interaction doesn't? Consider also: what would happen in year 10 if there were no contract enforcement ensuring the relationship ends? How might this affect behavior in earlier years? (Write 4-5 sentences)</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> This analysis assumes enforceable 10-year contracts that definitively end the relationship. Without such enforcement, year 10 becomes effectively a one-shot game where both companies would fish (no future to worry about). Knowing this, year 9 also unravels, and so on—a process called \"backward induction\" that can destroy cooperation even in repeated games with known endpoints.</p>",
