    "title": "Question 5: The Commons - First Multi-Player Game",
    "subtitle": "(Structured Analysis)",
    "read_html": "<h3>Teaching Box: Analyzing Multi-Player Games</h3>\n<p><strong>[READ]</strong> When analyzing games with n players, we need to think systematically about how individual choices aggregate:</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: Set up the decision</strong> Suppose there are n players total. You are one of them, so there are (n-1) others.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: Consider what others do</strong> If each of the (n-1) others chooses strategy xi (say, contributing xi dollars), then:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Total from others = (n-1) × xi</li>\n<li>If you choose yi, the grand total = (n-1)xi + yi</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Find your best response</strong> Given what others do, what yi maximizes your payoff?</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4: Look for symmetric equilibrium</strong> In symmetric equilibrium, everyone uses the same strategy, so xi = yi for all players.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> 4 people splitting a task. If 3 others each do 2 hours of work (6 hours total), and you do y hours, total work = 6 + y. You can then calculate your payoff based on this total.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Four farmers share a pasture. Each can graze 1 or 2 cattle. The pasture provides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Good nutrition if total cattle ≤ 5: 1 cow → 6 profit, 2 cows → 10 profit</li>\n<li>Adequate nutrition if total = 6-7: 1 cow → 4 profit, 2 cows → 6 profit</li>\n<li>Poor nutrition if total ≥ 8: 1 cow → 1 profit, 2 cows → 0 profit</li>\n</ul>",
