      "answer_b64": "",
      "note_html": "<p><strong>Note on Equilibrium Complexity (from the answer key):</strong> The original payoff structure creates an unstable game with no pure strategy Nash equilibrium. With the given payoffs, the system never settles—farmers would constantly want to switch strategies. While this instability itself is instructive (many commons do cycle through overuse and recovery), let's modify the payoffs slightly to create a clearer teaching example.</p>\n<p><strong>Modified Payoff Structure (used in the model answer):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Good nutrition if total ≤ 4: 1 cow → 8 profit, 2 cows → 12 profit</li>\n<li>Adequate nutrition if total = 5-6: 1 cow → 5 profit, 2 cows → 7 profit</li>\n<li>Poor nutrition if total ≥ 7: 1 cow → 2 profit, 2 cows → 0 profit</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The model answer below uses these modified payoffs, not the payoffs printed in the question.</p>"
