    "subtitle": "",
    "read_html": "<p><strong>Part I: From Cooperation to Coordination - Building Hardin's Insight (45 minutes)</strong></p>\n<p>[READ] In a prisoner's dilemma, both players have dominant strategies that lead to mutual harm. Consider two neighboring towns deciding whether to maintain a shared bridge:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Town B: Maintain</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Town B: Don't Maintain</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Town A: Maintain</strong></td>\n<td>(3,3)</td>\n<td>(-2,4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Town A: Don't Maintain</strong></td>\n<td>(4,-2)</td>\n<td>(-1,-1)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Understanding the Payoffs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Both Maintain (3,3)</strong>: Each town pays 5 units in maintenance costs but gets 8 units of bridge benefit = net 3</li>\n<li><strong>One Maintains, Other Doesn't (-2,4)</strong>:</li>\n<li>Maintainer: Pays full 5 units cost, gets only partial bridge benefit of 3 units = -2</li>\n<li>Non-maintainer: Pays nothing, gets partial bridge benefit of 4 units = 4</li>\n<li><strong>Neither Maintains (-1,-1)</strong>: Bridge deteriorates, each loses 1 unit from lack of access</li>\n<li>This creates the prisoner's dilemma: not maintaining is always individually better, but mutual maintenance is collectively optimal</li>\n</ul>",
