    "read_html": "<p><strong>Part II: Constitutional Types and Enforcement Mechanisms (45 minutes)</strong></p>\n<p>[READ] <strong>Hardin's Framework</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sovereign constitutions</strong> (nations, UN): No external enforcer. Stability comes from coordination - everyone follows because everyone expects everyone else to follow. Failure means chaos/civil war.</li>\n<li><strong>Contracted constitutions</strong> (corporations, clubs): Courts can enforce rules. Stability comes from legal remedies, BUT firms still fail through poor decisions or market forces.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Key difference: Sovereign constitutions can only be replaced through coordination on a new equilibrium. Contracted ones can be enforced by courts but may still collapse if the underlying business fails.</p>",
