    "read_html": "<p>[READ] The COVID-19 pandemic required unprecedented coordination between sovereign constitutions (governments) and contracted constitutions (pharmaceutical companies) to develop and distribute vaccines.</p>\n<p><strong>Normal times - Interests and behaviors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pharmaceutical companies: Want approval but fear lawsuits from adverse events</li>\n<li>Regulators: Fear approving dangerous drugs more than delaying good ones</li>\n<li>Different companies: Compete by exposing rivals' problems to gain market share</li>\n<li>Different countries: Maintain independent standards, skeptical of others' approvals</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Crisis times - Interests and behaviors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pharmaceutical companies: Want first-mover advantage, received liability protection</li>\n<li>Regulators: Fear blame for delays more than for approval errors</li>\n<li>Different companies: All want vaccine market to succeed, avoid undermining confidence</li>\n<li>Different countries: Want to show competence, avoid being last to approve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Consider the delegation structure that emerged:</p>\n<p><code>Citizens (want safe, effective vaccines)\n    ↓\nGovernments (sovereign constitution)\n    ↓ [Contracts + Regulation]\nPharmaceutical Companies (contracted constitution)\n    ↓ [Development]\nClinical Trials → Regulatory Agencies → Distribution\n    ↓\nPopulation Health Outcomes</code></p>",
