    "id": "q11",
    "title": "Question 11: Bureaucratic Pathology - Why Agencies Grow and Never Shrink",
    "subtitle": "",
    "read_html": "<p>[READ] Niskanen observed that bureaucracies systematically oversupply their services and grow beyond efficient size. Consider the Department of Agricultural Support, created in 1950 when 20% of citizens were farmers. Today, 2% are farmers, but the department has tripled in size and now runs urban nutrition programs, suburban lawn care advisories, and restaurant inspection protocols.</p>\n<p><strong>Niskanen's Core Logic - Quick Version:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Agencies know: True minimum cost = X</li>\n<li>Congress knows: Only what agency reports</li>\n<li>Agency claims: \"We need 2X to do the job properly\"</li>\n<li>Congress thinks: \"Maybe they need 1.5X, let's give them 1.8X\"</li>\n<li>Result: Agency gets 1.8X for job costing X = 80% waste</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The budget game works like this: The agency knows its true minimum cost to provide services but Congress doesn't. The agency requests the maximum budget Congress might approve. Congress can't verify if the budget is justified. Once established, programs create constituencies who lobby for continuation.</p>\n<p><strong>Compare three oversight mechanisms:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Mechanism</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Information Required</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Constituencies</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Congressional hearings</td>\n<td>Agency self-reports needs</td>\n<td>Program beneficiaries testify</td>\n<td>?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sunset provisions</td>\n<td>Programs expire unless renewed</td>\n<td>Beneficiaries mobilize at renewal</td>\n<td>?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Competing agencies</td>\n<td>Multiple agencies bid for same task</td>\n<td>Agencies undercut each other</td>\n<td>?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private contracting</td>\n<td>Compare government vs. market cost</td>\n<td>Contractors lobby for work</td>\n<td>?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>",
