    "subtitle": "",
    "read_html": "<p>[READ] The #MeToo movement revealed enforcement failures when behavior is unobservable. But solutions create new problems. Consider workplace harassment enforcement:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Approach</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Mechanism</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Benefits</strong></th>\n<th><strong>New Problems Created</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Traditional HR</td>\n<td>Require proof for action</td>\n<td>Due process protected</td>\n<td>Most real cases unprovable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Believe all claims\"</td>\n<td>Act on accusation alone</td>\n<td>More victims protected</td>\n<td>False accusations become weapons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open-door policies</td>\n<td>Never meet alone</td>\n<td>Reduces opportunities</td>\n<td>Destroys mentoring, especially for women</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Restorative justice</td>\n<td>Mediation and education</td>\n<td>Addresses power dynamics</td>\n<td>May enable continued abuse</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The paradox: Known power asymmetries that prevent reporting can be exploited in reverse - if everyone \"knows\" junior employees can't report, false accusations become more credible (\"they wouldn't dare unless true\").</p>",
