    "read_html": "<p><strong>Part V: Applied Design and Complex Cases (1.25 hours)</strong></p>\n<p>[READ] A large university is redesigning student government after a crisis of legitimacy. Last year saw fifteen percent voter turnout, a major embezzlement scandal in the social events budget, and protests that the government only represented domestic students from wealthy backgrounds while ignoring international students, working-class students, and part-time students. The administration threatens to dissolve student government entirely unless a new constitution can restore legitimacy and function. The university has thirty thousand students, including forty percent in STEM fields who primarily want more research funding and lab access, thirty-five percent in humanities and social sciences who prioritize cultural programming and speaker events, and twenty-five percent in professional schools who focus on career services and networking. International students comprise twenty-five percent across all schools but feel systematically excluded from leadership. Part-time and mature students, who make up twenty percent of the population, have never held a leadership position. The previous government collapsed when the president was caught hiring friends for paid positions without oversight.</p>\n<p>Three constitutional designs have been proposed. The first creates a Westminster-style parliamentary system where students elect fifty representatives by major and school, these representatives then elect a prime minister from among themselves, and the prime minister appoints cabinet ministers to run specific portfolios like academic affairs, social programming, and finance. The prime minister serves at the pleasure of parliament and can be removed by a no-confidence vote at any time.</p>\n<p>The second proposal establishes a commission government with five commissioners directly elected campus-wide, each running a specific portfolio such as academics, social life, finance, diversity, and student services. All major decisions require a three-out-of-five vote, preventing any single executive from dominating. Commissioners serve staggered two-year terms to ensure continuity.</p>\n<p>The third design creates a hybrid system with a directly elected president chosen by the entire campus and a separately elected thirty-member senate with representatives from each school. The president proposes initiatives and budgets while the senate must approve them. Both can claim to represent the will of the students, creating intentional tension and checks on power.</p>\n<p>[DO] Use this template for delegation chains:</p>\n<p><strong>Option A - Parliamentary:</strong></p>\n<p><code>30,000 Students\n    ↓ [vote by major/school]\n50 Representatives\n    ↓ [majority selects]\nPrime Minister\n    ↓ [appoints]\nCabinet Ministers (Academic, Social, Finance)\n    ↓ [implement]\nPolicies/Programs</code></p>\n<p><strong>Option B - Commission:</strong></p>\n<p><code>30,000 Students\n    ↓ [campus-wide vote for 5]\n5 Commissioners (parallel authority)\n    ↓ [3/5 vote required]\nJoint Decisions\n    ↓ [individual commissioners execute]\nPrograms in their domains</code></p>\n<p><strong>Option C - Hybrid:</strong></p>\n<p><code>30,000 Students\n    ↓ [campus-wide]        ↓ [by school]\nPresident              Senate (30)\n    ↓ [proposes]           ↓ [approves/rejects]\n         Joint Decisions (requires both)\n              ↓\n        Implementation</code></p>",
