  "intro_html": "<h3>Instructions for Students</h3>\n<p><strong>Time Expectation:</strong> This problem set is designed to take 6 hours of focused work. Work with classmates to understand concepts, but write your own responses.</p>\n<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> Pass/fail based on demonstrated effort and understanding. This is a teaching tool, not a test.</p>\n<p><strong>Materials Needed:</strong> Graph paper or digital drawing tool (Inkscape recommended), ruler, colored pens/pencils for spatial models.</p>\n<p><strong>Due:</strong> Wednesday Week 6, 15:00 (no grace period — Exam!)</p>\n<h2>Structure</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Part I: The Committee's Dilemma - Building Intuition</strong> (45 minutes) — Question 1</li>\n<li><strong>Part II: Arrow's Impossibility - From Intuition to Theory</strong> (60 minutes) — Questions 2–3</li>\n<li><strong>Part III: Spatial Models and the Median Voter</strong> (75 minutes) — Questions 3.5–6</li>\n<li><strong>Part IV: Information, Manipulation, and Strategic Voting</strong> (60 minutes) — Questions 7–8.5</li>\n<li><strong>Part V: Integration - Building and Breaking Democratic Institutions</strong> (90 minutes) — Questions 9–10</li>\n<li><strong>Part VI: Synthesis - The Tragic Beauty of Democracy</strong> (30 minutes) — Questions 11–12</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Appendix: Quick Reference</h2>\n<h3>Arrow's Conditions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rationality:</strong> All individuals have complete, transitive preferences</li>\n<li><strong>U (Universal Domain):</strong> The voting system must work for ANY possible combination of individual preferences</li>\n<li><strong>P (Pareto):</strong> If everyone prefers X to Y, society should prefer X to Y</li>\n<li><strong>D (Non-Dictatorship):</strong> No single person determines society's choice</li>\n<li><strong>I (Independence):</strong> Society's preference between X and Y depends only on individuals' preferences between X and Y</li>\n<li><strong>T (Transitivity):</strong> If society prefers X to Y and Y to Z, it must prefer X to Z</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Spatial Voting Concepts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ideal point:</strong> Most preferred position</li>\n<li><strong>Indifference curve:</strong> Points yielding equal utility</li>\n<li><strong>Winset:</strong> All points that beat status quo</li>\n<li><strong>Core:</strong> Point(s) with empty winset</li>\n<li><strong>Median voter:</strong> Decides outcome in 1D with single-peaked preferences</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Institutional Roles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Agenda setter:</strong> Controls what gets voted on</li>\n<li><strong>Veto player:</strong> Can block changes from status quo</li>\n<li><strong>Gate keeper:</strong> Decides whether proposals reach floor</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Key Theorems</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arrow:</strong> No perfect voting system exists</li>\n<li><strong>Black:</strong> Median voter theorem for 1D</li>\n<li><strong>McKelvey:</strong> Chaos in multidimensional voting</li>\n<li><strong>Gibbard-Satterthwaite:</strong> No strategy-proof voting except dictatorship</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>\"Democracy is the worst form of Government except for all those other forms that have been tried from time to time.\"</em> - Winston Churchill</p>\n<p>This quote captures the essence of social choice theory: democracy is mathematically imperfect but practically essential.</p>",
