      "prompt_html": "<p>Using majority rule, determine society's preferences:</p>\n<p>Profile A:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Action vs Comedy: _____</li>\n<li>Comedy vs Drama: _____</li>\n<li>Drama vs Action: _____</li>\n<li>Group ranking: _____</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Profile B:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Action vs Comedy: _____</li>\n<li>Comedy vs Drama: _____</li>\n<li>Drama vs Action: _____</li>\n<li>Group ranking: _____</li>\n</ul>",
      "answer_b64": "",
      "note_html": "<p><strong>Note on Problem Setup (from the answer key):</strong> There appears to be an issue with the preference profiles as stated in the original problem. To properly demonstrate an Independence violation, the social preference between Action and Comedy must reverse between Profile A and Profile B while no individual's Action vs. Comedy preference changes. The profiles given may not accomplish this. If your calculations show Action winning in both profiles, this indicates the problem setup needs adjustment. The key concept remains valid: Independence violations occur when society's X vs. Y preference changes even though nobody's individual X vs. Y preference changed—typically because preferences over a third option Z shifted.</p>"
