      "id": "q9a",
      "label": "Part A: Predict Instability",
      "kind": "DO",
      "prompt_html": "<p>Under simple majority rule, identify a cycle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>_</em> beats </strong><strong><em> with support from </em></strong>__</li>\n<li><strong><em>_</em> beats </strong><strong><em> with support from </em></strong>__</li>\n<li><strong><em>_</em> beats </strong><strong><em> with support from </em></strong>__</li>\n</ul>",
      "answer_b64": "",
      "note_html": "<p><strong>Note to Students (from the answer key):</strong> This question is optional and involves significant conceptual complexity. The problem setup contains an inherent tension that makes it challenging to work through cleanly.</p>\n<p><strong>The Conceptual Complication:</strong> The problem describes Balancers as having \"medium all\" preferences but also ranks them as \"E &gt; S &gt; C\". This creates tension between two interpretations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spatial interpretation:</strong> \"Medium all\" suggests their ideal point is at moderate levels on all three dimensions—perhaps (S=50, E=50, C=50) if we imagine a 0-100 scale. They want balance.</li>\n<li><strong>Ordinal interpretation:</strong> \"E &gt; S &gt; C\" suggests when forced to prioritize, they value Economic Development most, Security second, Climate third.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These aren't necessarily contradictory, but they require careful handling: a Balancer might have an ideal point at (S=50, E=50, C=50) in policy space, but when comparing two packages that differ on only one dimension, they use their E &gt; S &gt; C ranking; when comparing packages that differ on multiple dimensions, they calculate Euclidean distance from their ideal point.</p>\n<p><strong>How to approach this problem if you choose to do it:</strong> Think of groups as having two characteristics: location in 3D policy space (their ideal point) and priority weights when trading off dimensions. For Balancers: ideal point is centrist, but they weight E &gt; S &gt; C when evaluating trade-offs from that center.</p>"
